# Revisions git blame should skip, newest last. One full 40-character SHA per
# line; short SHAs are not accepted and fail loudly.
#
# GitHub reads this file by name and applies it to the blame view with no
# configuration. Locally it takes one command, once per clone:
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# Without that, `git blame` here attributes 423 lines of the Markdown corpus to
# a formatter rather than to whoever wrote them, and 54 of AGENTS.md's 462.
#
# Two rules for adding to this file, both learned rather than assumed.
#
# Only list a commit that changed formatting and NOTHING else. A listed commit
# becomes invisible to blame, so a substantive change buried in one would be
# attributed to whoever last touched the line for real, which is worse than the
# noise this file exists to remove. Verify before adding: normalise the diff for
# whatever the formatter is allowed to change and confirm nothing survives. That
# check is what caught #106's fence damage, and the entry below was not added
# until its repair was written.
#
# These SHAs survive because this repository merges pull requests rather than
# squashing them. A squash rewrites the commit, and an entry pointing at a SHA
# that no longer exists is skipped in silence rather than reported, so a squash
# merge of a formatting branch means the entry has to be re-derived from the
# commit that actually landed.

